  abstract: [
    This article develops an argument for gender abolition through a critique of gender identity informed by neuroscience and the philosophy of mind. I first argue that an identity defined through socially constructed gender categories depends on acquiring their social content. This conceptual argument is distinct from the question of how that content becomes part of an immediate sense of self. Drawing on predictive processing and experiments involving body-ownership illusions and altered footstep sounds, I propose an account of how culturally learned gender associations shape pre-reflective self-perception. I then argue that socially shared categories can enter recursive cultural learning and reinforce the combinations they name, giving those combinations an unequal capacity for cognitive reproduction. Multiplying labels does not by itself remove this asymmetry. On the proposed account, eliminating the associations that sustain contemporary pre-reflective gender identity would undermine its reproduction, although historical labels and voluntary affiliations could survive. Finally, I distinguish the disappearance of categories from the losses that a transition may impose on existing people. I defend gender abolition as a political project whose justification requires weighing these losses against the costs of continuing to reproduce gendered classifications, while recognising the experiences and healthcare needs of contemporary transgender people.
